WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.820
ROBERT KUBIS: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.560
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.470
So if you didn't see
"From Blobs to Tables,

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.670
Where to Store Your
Data," then stay tuned

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:10.940
because here is the Rewind.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.754
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.810
With all the choices available,
choosing the right storage

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.570
or database system for your
existing or new applications

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.740
can be hard.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.080
In their talk, Dave
Nettleton and Robert Saxby

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.060
walk you through
Google's cloud offerings

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.790
and help you make decisions
for the right storage servers

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.290
to use for your use cases.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.870
Starting with a
short retrospective

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.930
on how storing of bits and
bytes evolved over time,

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.600
Dave explains key
features of each product

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.360
from Cloud Memorystore
to Cloud Filestore.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.280
Driven by different
workload requirements,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.970
each product is used
in different ways

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.040
in various industries.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.920
So no matter if you
are building games

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.640
and need a fast
managed dictionary

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.110
with Cloud Memorystore for SAP
millisecond key value lookups,

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.120
or if you're running
your SAS platform

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.580
in retail and e-commerce
and need a fully managed

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.489
relational database
with Cloud SQL.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:06.770
And when your business
happens to grow like there's

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.480
no tomorrow, you'll need
a horizontally scalable

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.940
relational database.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:11.900
But no worry.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.140
We have you covered
here with Cloud Spanner.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.230
Of course, not all applications
require slick schemas

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.610
and relational semantics.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.760
Many times flexibility is
more important to move fast

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:23.960
and innovate.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.720
Our scalable document
database Cloud Firestore

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:27.980
has you covered here.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.140
With integration into
Firebase platform,

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.150
you can build your next highly
scalable mobile application

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.350
which synchronizes data across
multiple devices for millions

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.750
of concurrent users seamlessly.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.540
Now to make your applications
better behind the scenes,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.200
understanding how your
products are used is key.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.150
Cloud Bigtable with
its reads and writes

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.950
and single-digit
millisecond range

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.230
and age-based
compatibility will help

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.720
you drive your real-time
analytics to new levels.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.770
And if you need a massively
scalable pay-per-use OLAP data

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.680
warehouse that speaks SQL
and can create petabytes

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.800
of data in minutes,
then BigQuery

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.610
will be your best friend.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.490
But let's not forget, your
videos, music, images,

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.470
or for that matter
any kind of blobs,

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.110
all those go into planet
scale plop storage cloud

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.610
storage where you can optimize
for cost and access frequency

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.190
without changing APIs.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.620
To store bits and bytes
closer to your VMs,

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.590
we offer persistent disks
in various shapes and sizes,

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.830
spinning or solid
state, flexibly attached

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:31.460
to your virtual machines or
even closer next word processors

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.470
with local SSDs.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.750
We're almost there.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.550
There is one more storage
solution fresh from the press.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.340
If you need fast file access
from multiple machines,

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.250
for example distributed jobs
through a managed network

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:47.720
file system up to 64
terabytes and 30k re-dialups,

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.820
then check out our newly
added family member

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.600
Cloud Filestore Yes,
Cloud Filestore.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:53.990
The L is important here.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.150
In Toronto at the
session, Robert Saxby

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:59.060
talks about best practices and
common architecture patterns

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.010
for data storage and analytics.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.920
In short, separate your
compute and storage and you'll

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.000
do great.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.970
If this sparked your interest,
please watch the full talk.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.780
Links are in description below.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.110
Don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube channel

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.330
for more create
Next Rewind content.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.360
Thank you for watching.

